    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[# Eliminating Duplication in Templates

When I created my home, blog list, and blog post templates, I noticed the navigation bar was identical on every page. Copy-paste is the enemy of maintainability.

## The Problem

If I wanted to change the navigation tomorrow, I'd need to edit three files. What if I forget one? What if the files drift?

## The Solution: Partial Views

Razor partial views are reusable template fragments. I created `Navigation.cshtml` in `Views/Partials/` and referenced it from all three templates:

```razor
@Html.Partial("Navigation")
```

Now the navigation is defined once. If it needs to change, I update it in one place.

## Why This Matters

**Maintainability**: Changes propagate everywhere automatically.

**Consistency**: No accidental drift between versions of the same component.

**Clarity**: Templates are shorter, more readable, more focused.

## A Broader Lesson

The DRY principle isn't about laziness. It's about recognizing that duplicated code is a liability. Every duplicate is a future bug waiting to happen.

## Beyond Navigation

Partials work for any repeated component: headers, footers, sidebars, comment sections, etc. As the site grows, partials become essential.]]></Value>
    </content>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[Repeating the same navigation HTML on every page is a maintenance nightmare. Partial views taught me how to keep templates DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself).]]></Value>
